**Theorem 8.1 ($C^1$ and not $C^2$ in original strength).** The two physical branches have the same value and first strength derivative at the event, and different second strength derivatives.

Proof. Write $V_{\rm low}=\sum p_i|H_i|$. Differentiating moments and occupied contact values gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
V_{\rm low}'&=\sum p_i'|H_i|+\sum p_i\epsilon_iH_{i,\phi}\phi_i'\\
&=Y\cdot\frac d{d\lambda}\sum p_i\Gamma_i
-\lambda\frac d{d\lambda}\sum p_i\sigma_iu_i=-\lambda k'.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use $s=s_0\tau$, $\tau=k/\sqrt\alpha$, and $\mathcal T_A=(H_{\rm high}+V_{\rm low})/2$. The strict response of §6 gives the analytic strength inverse on each side and

$$
\mathcal T_s=-\frac{\lambda\sqrt\alpha}{2s_0},\qquad
\mathcal T_{ss}=-\frac{\sqrt\alpha}{2\delta\tau_\lambda}.
$$

The event law and multiplier are common. The implicit tangent data in §16 yield positive disjoint one-sided response intervals

$$
.0003369635<\tau'_{\rm six}<.0003369647,\qquad
.0003793181<\tau'_{\rm seven}<.0003793185.
$$

For the response records use the unweighted norm, $q=\|I-RJ\|_\infty<1$, $v=-RF_\lambda$, and $|X'_i-v_i|\le q\|v\|_\infty/(1-q)$. Their field `error` bounds this last expression and `Xprime` encloses each exact derivative. Applying the derivative of $k/\sqrt\alpha$ gives `tau_prime`. The six record uses the augmented $21$-variable order, the seven record the $22$-variable physical order, both on the exact event box with $p_6=0$ in the seven embedding. Therefore the values and first derivatives match, while the one-sided second derivatives differ. Continuity of branch derivatives to the event proves $C^1$ and failure of $C^2$. $\square$

